  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Expressed as a **mixed number** in lowest terms, $\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{1}{3}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{1}{3}$ is $\\frac{3}{1}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{1}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}\\times \\frac{3}{1}=\\frac{4\\times 3}{5\\times 1}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{5}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$",
